Below you’ll find a quick reference for **each** tool exposed via the `functions` namespace, together with a concrete example of how to *invoke* that tool.  
Feel free to copy‑paste the snippet into your own script – replace placeholder values (`<…>`) with real data relevant to your task.

| Tool | Purpose | Example Call |
|------|---------|--------------|
| **generate_image_tool** | Creates an image from a descriptive prompt and writes it to disk. | ```json
{
  "name": "generate_image_tool",
  "arguments": {
    "desc": "A serene sunrise over misty alpine peaks, golden light reflecting off a crystal clear lake.",
    "file_name": "sunrise_mountains.png"
  }
}
``` |
| **tts_tool** | Turns supplied text into spoken audio and stores it locally. | ```json
{
  "name": "tts_tool",
  "arguments": {
    "content": "Welcome to our guided meditation session!", 
    "speaker": "bria", 
    "file_name": "meditation_intro.mp3"
  }
}
``` |
| **write_markdown_tool** | Persists arbitrary markdown content to a `.md` file. | ```json
{
  "name": "write_markdown_tool",
  "arguments": {
    "content": "# Project Overview\nThis project aims to...", 
    "filename": "overview.md"
  }
}
```
 |
| **write_html_tool** | Saves raw HTML markup to a local file. | ```json
{
  "name": "write_html_tool",
  "arguments": {
    "content": "<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>Hello</title></head><body><h1>Hi!</h1></body></html>", 
    "filename": "hello_world.html"
  }
}
``` |
| **search_web_tool** | Performs a DuckDuckGo lookup and retrieves a handful of snippets. | ```json
{
  "name": "search_web_tool",
  "arguments": {
    "query": "latest trends in renewable energy 2026", 
    "num_results": 5,
    "source": "news"
  }
}
``` |
| **get_url_content** | Fetches plain text from a target webpage. | ```json
{
  "name": "get_url_content",
  "arguments": {
    "url": "https://example.org/about-us", 
    "timeout": 20,
    "user_agent": "MyCustomAgent/2.0"
  }
}
``` |

### How to actually run a tool

If you're working inside an environment where the agent has direct access to the underlying API:

```python
import json
from mytoolkit import call_function   # hypothetical helper

payload = {
    "name": "tts_tool",
    "arguments": {"content":"Hello world!", "speaker":"alice"}
}

response = call_function(json.dumps(payload))
print(response)
```

Replace `"call_function"` with whatever mechanism your platform uses to dispatch the JSON payload to the appropriate backend service.

